# FOCUSED — oodar substrate audit (v4.0.24, HEAD 228e747+fix)
File: oodar/audit/2026-09-11-focused-oodar.oot

Scope: oodar only. Method: hostile re-verification on this host —
rebuilt liboodar.a from current source (the shipped .a was Sep 9),
ran `make test` (3 lints + 17 challengers, contract/wal/ffi
double-run), read the confinement + cap core by hand.

# Verdict

The substrate holds. `make test` green on rebuilt source: all 3
structural lints, 67 cap=0 mutator refusals, cap_escape 0/4,
cap_threat 0/4, pathcap, differential_cap, sandbox_containment
(live Landlock deny on /etc/passwd), proc_mem gate, env_pwd,
fuzz 200 iters. Cap tokens are 64-bit getentropy per process,
pthread_once, explicit_bzero'd at exit, aborts with no fallback.
sys_exec_wait is fork+execvp (no shell), argv per-element dup,
child env scrubbed to OODA_/OO_/PWD with a fixed PATH. fs
confinement canonicalizes via realpath on both sides, refuses "/"
segments, uses dirfd-pinned openat with O_NOFOLLOW on the leaf.
landlock_restrict.c calls the real __NR_landlock_* syscalls under
PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS.

# Findings fixed in this pass

F1 (MAJOR, hardening): the runtime shipped with zero stack
canaries. `nm lib/liboodar.a` showed no __stack_chk references and
a fresh compile confirmed it — this host's gcc defaults
-fstack-protector OFF and the Makefile never requested it. Every
fs-boundary function copies untrusted OoStr bytes into PATH_MAX
stack buffers (fs_lowlevel.c to_cpath callers). Fixed:
CFLAGS += -fstack-protector-strong (explicit, not toolchain luck).
Rebuilt: build/oodar.o now carries __stack_chk. Suite re-green.

F2 (MINOR): scripts/Makefile had no working CWD. REPO_ROOT
defaulted to `cd ..` (right only from scripts/), while
BUILD_DIR/LIB_DIR were bare relatives (right only from repo root).
Result: `make -f scripts/Makefile` from the repo root resolved
REPO_ROOT to the polyrepo root and failed; `make clean` from
scripts/ deleted scripts/build (nothing) and left build/oodar.o
in place — a silent clean no-op, observed live (Sep 9 object
archived into a "fresh" lib). Fixed: both anchored on
$(lastword $(MAKEFILE_LIST)) — every target now works from any
CWD. Verified: clean+rebuild from repo root, no overrides.

# Corrections to prior claims

- std audit addendum M6 said the oo_fs_mkdir deny-vs-EEXIST
  ordering "needs oodar to EEXIST-tolerate before the boundary
  check". Wrong: that ordering would mint an existence oracle —
  oo_fs_mkdir(fw, "/etc") returning Ok iff /etc is a dir leaks
  out-of-bounds path existence, while existence probing is
  deliberately FsReadCap+read-confine gated. Deny-first is the
  correct fail-closed order; fs_mkdir_p's absolute-path failure is
  intended confinement, not a runtime bug. Working convention
  stands: workspace-relative or in-root paths only.

# Hardening notes (not defects)

- oo_cap_self_token(i) returns any of the 26 cap tokens by index at
  C linkage (caps.c:182) — diagnostic surface for the differential
  test. Unreachable from .oo (no extern syntax; FfiCap is the only
  native-call lane and it is its own trust boundary), but a static
  + qa-only wrapper would close the C-ABI export for free.
- OODA_NO_JAIL=1 disables all fs confinement (fs_lowlevel.c:104)
  and survives oo_child_filter_env (OODA_ prefix is kept), so
  sys_exec children inherit the disabled jail. Deliberate debug
  hatch, but worth a comment noting the propagation is intended.
- oo_process_policy_getenv allowlists OODA_/OO_/OODACODEX/PWD and
  oo_env_get is EnvCap-gated — but there is no oo_env_set anywhere
  in the surface, so confined .oo code cannot set OODA_NO_JAIL
  itself. Verified: grep finds no setenv/putenv outside the fixed
  PATH write in the child filter.

